\section{Before and after}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Neither method makes the data less noisy.} The error of the load
measurement carries straight into every taxon (Vandeputte et al. 2017;
Props et al. 2017): measure load in replicate.
\item \strong{16S copy-number correction} (dividing each taxon's counts by its 16S
gene copy number) is a separate, earlier step in Vandeputte's pipeline,
and isn't part of either function. Do it with \code{sweep()} (see the
examples). The authors' script then rounds the corrected table \strong{up}
with \code{ceiling()}; \code{qmp_rarefy()} requires whole numbers instead, so call
\code{qmp_rarefy(ceiling(x), load)} to do as the script does.
\item \strong{Spike-ins:} the load is usually
\code{spike_added * total_reads / spike_reads}. Drop the spike-in feature
from \code{counts} before calling \verb{qmp_*()}.
\item \strong{The results are absolute abundances, not compositions.} Don't follow
them with a centred log ratio (\code{norm = 'clr'}), which removes the load
again. Bray-Curtis on QMP data is the usual next step: \code{\link[=bray]{bray()}} or
\code{\link[=beta_div]{beta_div()}} with \code{norm = 'none'}.
}
}

\section{Predicted loads}{


Without a measured load, one can be predicted from a fecal profile with
the MLP tool of Nishijima et al. (2025;
\url{https://microbiome-tools.embl.de/mlp/}; an R package not on CRAN):
\code{pred <- MLP(profile, profiler, training_data, "load")} gives cells per
gram, to pass as \code{load}: \code{qmp_scale(counts, pred$load)}, with \code{counts}'
samples in the same order as \code{profile}'s. MLP's own \code{"qmp"} output is
the same calculation as \code{qmp_scale()}. But:
\itemize{
\item \strong{A prediction is not a measurement.} It is computed from the same
relative profile, so it adds no independent information, its errors
are tied to composition, and it can't confirm a compositional result
(ecodive's reasoning). Predicted and measured loads correlated at
\eqn{r \approx} 0.67–0.68 within, and 0.56 across, the training
cohorts.
\item \strong{Human stool only}, profiled with the supported tools (mOTUs,
MetaPhlAn, DADA2 + RDP), and trained on European cohorts.
\item That load is "the major determinant of gut microbiome variation" is a
reason to measure or adjust for load, not a validation of any one
prediction.
}
}

\section{Differences from QMP.R}{


\code{qmp_rarefy()} follows \code{rarefy_even_sampling_depth()} in the authors'
\code{QMP.R} (BSD-3, © 2017 Raes Lab), except:
\itemize{
\item It doesn't apply \code{ceiling()} to \code{counts}; non-integer counts are an
error.
\item Targets \eqn{s_{min} L_i} are floored (as the script's call to
\code{phyloseq::rarefy_even_depth()} does), but a value within
\eqn{10^{-9}} (relative) of a whole number counts as that number, so
that floating-point rounding never costs a read; the sample with the
smallest sampling depth keeps all its reads.
\item A sample whose target is 0 reads (a load so small that
\eqn{s_{min} L_i < 1}) is an error, naming it, where the script returns
\code{NaN}s. So is an empty sample.
\item Rarefaction uses ecodive's own \code{\link[=rarefy]{rarefy()}} code and \code{seed}, so the
random draws differ from the script's (\code{rngseed = 711}), while the
sampling depths, targets, and totals are the same.
}
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
